A||||7||BBBAD GT||Subwoofer help||||1||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||02-13-2000||12:23 AM||||I don't think the subwoofer is working correctly in my 87 GT.  The factory stereo in the Fiero isn't the highline model (no equalizer, only seperate bass and trebble slide buttons), but the subwoofer does emit a bass sound, nothing what a subwoofer should sound like tho.  Its not powerful either.  If I turn up the subwoofer to max and the bass to max, then it gets all 'bass distorted', but not a powerful thump that I was expecting.  Any ideas on what to dig into first?  The sound is fine though the radio (except the tumping bass I expected), so I don't think its the amp.  Any experiences like this?  Thanks for the help.<BR> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/fiero1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/fiero1.jpg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||02-13-2000||01:10 AM||||Those were light-weight, cheap-ass little subwoofers to begin with, so after 13 years of use, it sure don't surprise me that it might be toast.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||02-13-2000||02:59 AM||||BB,<P>Have no fear... no on with the Performance Sound option gets a thump out of their extra speaker.  You definitely are not going to get a "thump" out of any 5.25" woofer of any brand.  It is physically incapable of producing low frequencies (regardless of what BOSE claims).  Through the use of an enclosure, Pontiac has tried to fool the sound into thinking it was created by a larger speaker than it was and done so remarkably well if you ask me.  <P>If you want to "thump" you need something that has a frequency response rating down to around 20HZ.  This is generally a bogus # since it is only capable of reproducing sound at that frequency under "optimal conditions".  But an average 5.25" should only be able to reproduce sounds in the 5000 HZ range (I could be a little off on this #).  <P>I would imagine that the distortion that you are hearing is because you are reaching the clipping point for that speaker.  This is caused by trying to play the speaker too loud for the power it is given.  Simply replacing the amplifier with a more powerful one should help to alleviate this problem.  Bat is correct though that the speaker quality was not good initially and needs to be replaced by now.  However, if you continue to listen at or above the clipping point what speaker you have left will soon be toast.  <P>Best of luck to you...<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000003||stimpy||02-13-2000||10:15 AM||||The Performance Sound option is a joke.  My 2 cents is get yourself a set of Aura Bass Shakers. These mount to the floor under the seat and provide real thump. They don't look very impressive and you can't hear them from 2 blocks away, so they really don't sell that well. If you can find them, you should be able to get a good deal. And you can always sell your subwoofer setup on ebay for $500 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||02-13-2000||12:05 PM||||You guys have been telling me what I initially suspected.  I shouldn't be expecting the world with this 13 yr old car, and realistically, I listen to the exhaust note more than the radio anyway.  I guess my memory of the subwoofers first impressions was 14 yrs ago in highschool.  Those highschool ears probably thought it was the best thing since the remote (or fake ID).  <P>I might try that Ebay thing, I personally wouldn't pay over $50 for it, but if somebody is willing to pay $500............<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/fiero1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/fiero1.jpg</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||02-14-2000||06:19 PM||||Mine has an aftermarket radio with bass and treble adjusts. I've found the factory sub really distorts if I turn up the bass on the radio. It sounds much better with the radio bass in the middle position. <P>I think the amp output is only about 20 watts which is not going to "thump" very much through any speaker. It just gives a decent bass sound for a factory stereo from the 80s. If you want thump, you gotta go aftermarket.<P>David.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||02-14-2000||07:24 PM||||If you want bump you can easily install a 10" sub in the front of the car where the spare tire is.  Then relocate the spare to the rear.  This is the easiet way I've seen yet.  But the best I've seen is fierosounds.IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||02-14-2000||08:40 PM||||In my car I removed the stock subwoofer due to it's poor sound. I now have a "Bazooka Tube" It has an 80 watt amp built in. They come in 3 sizes 6 1/2, 8, and 10. I have the 6 1/2 so the passenger seat needs to be moved forward 1 click. It rattles the car if you crank it up but during normal levels it sounds great. I have tried the "Truck" style boxes and they are OK but a little "boomy"<BR>I like how Fierosound did his. A friend is going to be doing that to his car so I'll see how it sounds when it's done.||||reg||1
